  "query_preview": "..An area of high pressure over New Mexico is starting\nto drift westward today through Tuesday before becoming centered \nover Arizona. As we move through the rest of the week, the high \nstarts to sink further to the south and east. \n\nToday and Tuesday...Shower activity lifted north this morning,\nwhich was in response to outflows from storms across the valley \novernight. Increased cloud cover as a result is limiting\ninstability as evidenced in the 18Z sounding, with most \nthunderstorm activity early this afternoon over western Coconino \ncounty where there was partial clearing. Going through the rest of\ntoday, we see monsoon moisture remaining in place with another \nfairly active afternoon/evening across the region. Best chances \nwill be across the higher terrain and across western portions of \nthe forecast area as moisture is advected up from the Gulf of \nCalifornia in the continued southerly flow. Storm motions remain \non the slower side which will keep flash flooding as the main \nhazard for the day. As the high pushes back to the west, we will \nlose some of that good moisture advection into the region and \nstorm chances will start to decrease. Expect storms to develop \nmainly across the higher terrain for Tuesday.\n\nWednesday through Friday...As the high becomes centered over \nArizona, we see the moisture start to get cut off as subsidence wins \nout with drier conditions expected. Best chances for any storms will \nremain mainly across the higher terrain with the highest chances \ntowards the White Mountains. The other factor of the strengthening \nhigh will be the increasing temperatures, which will be hot across \nthe region. With highs reaching back into the 100s and heat risk \nincreasing, we have issued an Extreme Heat Warning for the lower \nelevations of the Grand Canyon and Yavapai County, for areas below \n4000 feet, in effect for Wednesday through Friday.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...Models show the high elongating and sliding to \nthe east of the region by the weekend. This shift may help to usher \nmoisture back into our area with better chances for showers and \nthunderstorms returning along with slightly cooler temperatures.",
